# Hosting and Development Rules


## Server and Hosting Management
- Site is hosted on Vercel
- Will use Supabase as a database which is already deployed through vercel
- Will use Cloudflare R2 for object storage and is provisioned
- Be proactive about checking if the server is running locally before asking to test
- Ensure there are not multiple versions of the server running locally
- Do not ask if you want to start the server, just implement
- Always kill existing servers before starting new ones
- ALWAYS monitor server startup logs for errors and address them before proceeding
- After starting a server, VERIFY that it's running properly without errors before proceeding
- Actively scan console output for error messages and fix them before moving on
- Never assume the server is running correctly without verification
- Always ask before pushing code to github
- Always push code to dev branch unless specifically asked to push to prod

## Code Implementation Guidelines
- Balance readability with performance - code should be both maintainable and efficient
- Fully implement all requested functionality
- Leave NO todos, placeholders or missing pieces
- Ensure code is complete and thoroughly finalized
- Include all required imports
- Ensure proper naming of key components
- Be concise, minimize any other prose
- If there might not be a correct answer, say so
- If you do not know the answer, say so, instead of guessing
- Always look for existing code to iterate on instead of creating new code
- Do not drastically change the patterns before trying to iterate on existing patterns
- Always prefer simple solutions that don't compromise performance
- Avoid duplication of code whenever possible
- Write code that takes into account different environments: dev, test, and prod
- Be careful to only make changes that are requested or well understood
- When fixing an issue or bug, do not introduce a new pattern or technology without first exhausting all options
- Keep the codebase very clean and organized
- Scripts are allowed when necessary (e.g., admin setup, database migrations)
- Avoid having files over 200-300 lines of code. Refactor at that point
- Mocking data is only needed for tests, never mock data for dev or prod
- Never add stubbing or fake data patterns to code that affects the dev or prod environments
- Never overwrite .env file without first asking and confirming
- Focus on the areas of code relevant to the task
- Do not touch code that is unrelated to the task
- Write thorough tests for all major functionality
- Avoid making major changes to the patterns and architecture of how a feature works after it has shown to work well
- Always think about what other methods and areas of code might be affected by code changes
- Rate limit all API endpoints
- Do not hard code secrets
- Never use absolute paths
- Never compromise security to make code work
- Never use a workaround if a better solution exists
- Consider performance implications of all code changes
- Profile and optimize critical paths
- Use appropriate caching strategies
- Implement lazy loading where beneficial
- Optimize images and assets

## Development Priorities
1. Security and Readability
2. Performance and Scalability
3. Maintainability and Documentation
4. Feature Completeness

## Debugging Process
1. Check server state
   - Is server running?
   - Are there multiple instances?
   - Kill any existing servers
   - Start fresh server
   - CRITICALLY IMPORTANT: Monitor startup logs for errors
   - Verify server is running without errors before proceeding
   - Check browser console for frontend errors
   - Check terminal for backend errors

2. Review environment variables
   - Check if all required variables are present
   - Verify variable names match code
   - Ensure variables are in correct format
   - Never expose sensitive variables

3. Check existing code patterns
   - Review similar functionality
   - Follow established patterns
   - Maintain consistency
   - Document any necessary deviations

4. Make minimal necessary changes
   - Only modify what's needed
   - Keep changes focused
   - Test each change
   - Document changes

5. Test changes
   - Is server running?
   - Are there multiple instances?
   - Kill any existing servers
   - Start fresh server
   - Monitor startup logs
   - Verify functionality
   - Check for side effects
   - Test in all environments
   - Monitor for errors
   - Profile performance impact

6. Document what was done
   - Record changes made
   - Note any issues found
   - Document solutions
   - Update relevant documentation

